-   - **Add a reference where it confirms a specific behaviour.** When an edit says how a named package behaves, link the package's own material: its documentation, release notes, an issue, a test, or a line of source. A repository named under [AWESOME-HUMANS.md: Named references](../../AWESOME-HUMANS.md#named-references) qualifies too. Look for it in the `<id>`, `microsoft`, and `fsharp` organisations on GitHub, and let the [admission criteria](https://github.com/awesome-humans/website/blob/main/pages/conventions/admission-criteria.md#reserved-ids-markings-and-references) decide what qualifies. Read a reference before you link it, and link it only if it says what the claim says. It never goes in `sources:`. A reference fact, as the admission criteria define it, needs no roster citation. For a choice or a judgement, it sits beside the roster citation and never replaces it. Pin a link to a line of code to a commit.
-   - **Check a contradiction against the package's own material.** When a post contradicts an opinion about how something behaves, read the package's documentation, tests, or source before you choose. If they settle it, correct the opinion, and cite a roster source that agrees, or that material as a reference. Link the evidence in the pull request.
